### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants you to reverse a string using the Stream API and StringBuilder. This tests your understanding of `String.chars()`, the three-argument `collect`, and why build-then-reverse beats insert-at-zero.

### How do you reverse a string with StringBuilder (idiomatic)?

The simplest and most efficient approach uses StringBuilder directly.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class StringReverseIdiomatic {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String accountRef = "SAV2001-HDFC";

        String reversed = new StringBuilder(accountRef)
            .reverse()
            .toString();

        System.out.println("Original: " + accountRef);
        System.out.println("Reversed: " + reversed);
    }
}
```

```text
Original: SAV2001-HDFC
Reversed: CFDH-1002VAS
```

### How do you reverse a string using chars() and a stream?

The stream approach collects characters into a StringBuilder using the three-argument `collect(supplier, accumulator, combiner)`, then reverses.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class StringReverseStream {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String accountRef = "SAV2001-HDFC";

        String reversed = accountRef.chars()
            .mapToObj(c -> (char) c)
            .collect(
                StringBuilder::new,
                StringBuilder::append,
                StringBuilder::append)
            .reverse()
            .toString();

        System.out.println("Reversed: " + reversed);
    }
}
```

```text
Reversed: CFDH-1002VAS
```

The three-argument collect takes a supplier (`StringBuilder::new`), an accumulator (`append` each character), and a combiner (for parallel merging). After collecting, one call to `reverse()` flips the entire sequence.

### Why is build-then-reverse better than insert-at-zero?

| Approach | Time complexity | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| Build forward + reverse once | O(n) | Append is amortized O(1), reverse is O(n) |
| Insert at index 0 each time | O(n squared) | Each insert shifts all existing characters right |
| String concatenation in loop | O(n squared) | Each concat creates a new immutable String |

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class ReversePerformance {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String code = "SAV2001";

        // WRONG: quadratic insert at zero
        StringBuilder slow = new StringBuilder();
        for (char c : code.toCharArray()) {
            slow.insert(0, c);  // shifts all existing chars every time
        }
        System.out.println("Slow: " + slow);

        // CORRECT: build forward, reverse once
        StringBuilder fast = new StringBuilder();
        for (char c : code.toCharArray()) {
            fast.append(c);  // amortized O(1)
        }
        fast.reverse();
        System.out.println("Fast: " + fast);
    }
}
```

```text
Slow: 1002VAS
Fast: 1002VAS
```

Both produce the same result, but insert-at-zero is quadratic because every insert shifts the existing content.

### What about Unicode surrogate pairs?

StringBuilder.reverse() is surrogate-aware. Characters outside the Basic Multilingual Plane are stored as two `char` values (a surrogate pair). A naive character-by-character reversal would split the pair and corrupt the text.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SurrogateReversal {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Musical symbol (U+1D11E) stored as surrogate pair
        String text = "A\uD834\uDD1EB";
        System.out.println("Original length: " + text.length());

        // StringBuilder.reverse() keeps the pair intact
        String safe = new StringBuilder(text).reverse().toString();
        System.out.println("Safe reverse: " + safe);
    }
}
```

```text
Original length: 4
Safe reverse: B𝄞A
```

For plain ASCII account codes any method works, but the built-in reverse is the safe default.

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake 1: using string concatenation in a loop.

```java
// WRONG: creates a new String on every iteration
String code = "SAV2001";
String reversed = "";
for (int i = code.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    reversed += code.charAt(i);  // new String each time
}

// CORRECT: use StringBuilder
String correct = new StringBuilder(code).reverse().toString();
```

Mistake 2: forgetting that chars() returns int values, not char.

```java
// WRONG: prints int code points
"ABC".chars().forEach(c -> System.out.print(c + " "));
// Output: 65 66 67

// CORRECT: cast to char
"ABC".chars()
    .mapToObj(c -> (char) c)
    .forEach(c -> System.out.print(c + " "));
// Output: A B C
```

### What about a pure stream reduce approach?

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class ReverseWithReduce {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String code = "SAV2001";

        String reversed = code.chars()
            .mapToObj(c -> String.valueOf((char) c))
            .reduce("", (a, b) -> b + a);

        System.out.println("Reduced: " + reversed);
    }
}
```

```text
Reduced: 1002VAS
```

This works but creates many intermediate strings because each reduce step concatenates. Use the StringBuilder approach for production code.

### How is this different from using codePoints()?

| Method | Returns | Handles surrogates |
|---|---|---|
| chars() | IntStream of char values | No, splits surrogate pairs |
| codePoints() | IntStream of Unicode code points | Yes, each surrogate pair is one int |

For reversing strings with supplementary characters, prefer `codePoints()` with `Character.toChars()` to rebuild each code point correctly.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you reverse only the words in a sentence, not the characters?" Split by space, reverse the array, and join.
- "How do you check if a string is a palindrome?" Reverse and compare, or use the index-based approach.
- "What is the time complexity of StringBuilder.reverse()?" O(n), because it swaps characters from both ends toward the middle.
